+# Decision: Resolving concurrent visit edits — whole-visit revisions
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers on shared tablets correct the same visit independently, sometimes days apart, after comparing field notes; tablets can be offline for 48 hours, so a worker's starting state can be stale by the time a submission arrives. The record store must decide what "the current visit" means when competing submissions exist. The server cannot infer which field changes are scientifically related — a species-identification fix and its cover estimate sit in different fields but belong to the same judgment.
+
+Whatever mechanism is chosen, two constraints are inherited and every option respects them (background, not decision elements): every original submission is preserved with its author and receipt time, and no submitted edit is ever silently discarded (HLD tenets). The failure being replaced is the team's current spreadsheet regime, where imports overwrite corrections without showing which submission was lost.
+
+This decision produces the contract two downstream nodes build on: tablet-sync's local state and submission behavior, and review-export's coordinator interface and export eligibility.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Silent-failure blast radius (major).** What happens when the mechanism is wrong. Derives from the tenet *do not silently discard a submitted edit*, extended to *do not silently distort one*: a failure that overwrites or miscombines an observation without any human seeing it is the worst outcome this system can produce, because it corrupts scientific data while looking legitimate.
+- **Review workload for two coordinators (major).** Derives from the tenet *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. The team's correction pattern — workers compare notes, then correct the same visit — makes concurrent edits routine rather than rare, so whatever generates review work will generate it every week.
+- **Path inspectability (major).** Derives from the tenet *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: every value in the Friday CSV should be traceable to one worker's submission or one coordinator's decision, without bespoke provenance machinery.
+- **Export readiness by Friday (moderate).** Visits with unresolved review work cannot enter the export; a mechanism that leaves many visits unresolved near the deadline delays the science partner's data.
+- **Rule maintenance burden (moderate).** A mechanism that depends on merge rules accumulates latent logic — for scalar fields, observer lists, species entries — that must be specified, tested, and kept correct as the record schema evolves, with no systems administrator to own it.
+- **Worker experience (moderate).** Field workers must understand what happened to their corrections; a mechanism whose handling of a routine correction feels like disappearance will stop workers from correcting at all.
+- **Tablet simplicity (minor).** Visit payloads are small, so either wire format is affordable; this criterion breaks ties only.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission is a complete, self-contained revision of one visit, naming the revision it started from. Concurrent edits compete as whole revisions: a coordinator compares the competing payloads and selects one to become current; nothing is ever combined by software. A coordinator who needs parts of two submissions picks one and submits the combined correction as a new revision through the standard submission path, with the coordinator as author. Tablets track one piece of state per visit — the base revision ID — and send complete payloads.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species; both submit from revision 4. Two competing revisions appear in the coordinator's queue; the coordinator selects one (or selects and submits a combined follow-up). The worker whose revision was not selected sees, via the acceptance outcome, that their edit went to review — it is never lost.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Silent-failure blast radius: **strong** — there are no rules to be wrong; every conflict surfaces as review work a human decides.
+- Review workload: **weak** — even unrelated single-field corrections from the same base produce competing revisions, so the queue grows with every concurrent correction.
+- Path inspectability: **strong** — every exported value traces to one complete submission or one recorded coordinator selection.
+- Export readiness: **weak** — more visits sit unresolved, and each unresolved visit is held out of the Friday CSV.
+- Rule maintenance: **strong** — no merge rules exist to maintain.
+- Worker experience: **partial** — unrelated corrections land in review, which feels heavier; the acceptance outcome keeps it from feeling like disappearance.
+- Tablet simplicity: **strong** — base revision IDs plus complete payloads, no client-side diff machinery.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a larger coordinator queue for the guarantee that no combination happens outside a human's decision.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Tablets keep a base snapshot of each visit and send structured change sets naming the changed fields. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically into a new revision; changes to the same field go to review, presented with field-level provenance. Review volume shrinks to genuine same-field conflicts, routine corrections apply without coordinator effort, and more visits are export-ready sooner.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A's observer-name fix and worker B's added species merge cleanly with no review work; only a same-field collision — both revising a cover estimate — reaches the coordinator.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Silent-failure blast radius: **weak** — the merge rules' central failure is invisible by construction: two disjoint field edits that are scientifically related (a species-identification fix and its cover estimate) combine automatically, and the miscombined record looks exactly like a legitimate one. No review step sees it.
+- Review workload: **strong** — only overlapping changes generate review work.
+- Path inspectability: **weak** — a combined revision's story spans multiple authors; explaining an exported value requires field-level provenance machinery the first release would have to build and maintain.
+- Export readiness: **strong** — concurrently edited visits resolve automatically unless a genuine same-field conflict exists.
+- Rule maintenance: **weak** — scalar, observer-list, and species-entry merge rules must each be specified, tested, and kept correct as the schema evolves, with no administrator to own them.
+- Worker experience: **strong** — routine corrections apply immediately; partial — workers whose edits were silently combined with others cannot see that it happened either.
+- Tablet simplicity: **partial** — base snapshots and change-set construction add client machinery, though payloads remain small.
+
+**Summary.** Trades the guarantee against invisible combination for a much smaller review queue and faster export readiness.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The two failure modes decide it. Whole-visit revisions fail by producing too much review work — visible, bounded, and operable by two coordinators: it costs coordinator time. Field-aware merge fails by silently combining scientifically related edits — invisible exactly when it happens, corrupting data while looking legitimate: it costs data integrity. The team judges the second failure intolerable and the first merely expensive. The decision turns on the tenets *do not silently discard a submitted edit* (extended to *do not silently distort one*) and *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*; it is taken against the two-coordinators tenet, accepting a heavier coordinator workload while staying within what two people can operate.
+
+**Why the other approach remains credible.** The judgment flips if its premises do:
+
+- Field data shows concurrent corrections are overwhelmingly disjoint and rarely scientifically coupled — making the invisible-combination risk too rare to fund the queue cost.
+- Coordinator review volume measurably threatens the Friday export, with visits repeatedly arriving too late for the CSV.
+- Merge rules can be constrained to fields whose scientific couplings are enumerated and signed off by the science partner — changing silent failure from *possible by construction* to *bounded and audited*.
+- Requiring manual approval of each combined record becomes acceptable; this strips merge's queue advantage while keeping its disjoint-field semantics, and is the natural first revisiting point if rule quality is ever in doubt.
+
+**What tablet-sync must accommodate.**
+
+- Retain each local visit's base revision ID and submit complete payloads in the record-store envelope.
+- Interpret and display the acceptance outcome — became current, or stored as competing on an unresolved visit — so a worker whose edit went to review does not experience it as disappearance.
+- Decide whether to show pending competing revisions before a worker edits from a base that may not win (a tablet-sync design decision, not settled here).
+- Retry safely through the idempotency-key contract.
+
+**What review-export must accommodate.**
+
+- A whole-revision comparison view — complete competing payloads side by side, not field merges — with a selection operation matching the record-store resolution contract.
+- Export eligibility restricted to resolved visits, with unresolved visits held out of the Friday CSV; the queue includes unrelated-field competitions by design, and coordinator capacity should be sized for that pattern.
+- The follow-up correction path for coordinators combining observations: a standard submission that creates a new revision with the coordinator as author.
+
+**What this forecloses.** Field-level provenance — provenance is per submission, not per field; an audit trail of who changed one field is not built and cannot be retrofitted without new machinery. Automatic export readiness for concurrently edited visits. Any future merge capability arrives as a new decision layered on the retained revision history, not as configuration of this one.
